+# The Daily Grind
+
+A coffee shop account application with real WebAuthn passkeys for account registration, passwordless sign-in, management, and reauthentication.
+
+## Run
+
+Use Node.js 24 or later and pnpm.
+
+```sh
+pnpm install
+pnpm start
+```
+
+The app listens on port `11222` (or `PORT`). `pnpm start` builds the local browser bundle before starting the Express server. Visit `http://localhost:11222` for local passkey development. WebAuthn needs localhost or HTTPS, and a compatible browser and authenticator. Opening the HTML file directly will not provide authentication.
+
+For deployment, set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` to the exact externally accessible origin, such as `https://coffee.example.com`, without a trailing slash. The relying party ID is that origin’s hostname. The server trusts one reverse proxy hop; configure that proxy to supply the correct Host and X-Forwarded-Proto headers. Use HTTPS and persistent storage. Passkeys created for one domain cannot be used on a different domain.
+
+Account records, credential public keys, signature counters, and sessions persist in `data/accounts.sqlite`. Override this location with `DATABASE_PATH`. Back up this database; mount the data directory on a persistent volume in a container. Never commit account data. This implementation uses one SQLite-backed server instance.
+
+## Account flows
+
+- **Registration:** Create a profile and passkey using a face, fingerprint, screen-lock PIN, or security key. The account is only stored after server-side WebAuthn verification succeeds. Resident credentials and user verification are required.
+- **Authentication:** Sign in with a discoverable passkey, including a nearby phone or security key. The server checks the challenge, origin, relying party ID, signature, and user verification; updates the signature counter; and rotates the session cookie.
+- **Management:** List, add, rename, and remove your own passkeys. Sensitive changes require verification from the previous five minutes. Up to ten passkeys are allowed. The last passkey cannot be deleted. Removing a key ends other sessions.
+- **Reauthentication:** Verify using one of your account’s registered passkeys before sensitive actions. You can also explicitly verify from the Active sessions tab and revoke other sessions.
+
+Challenges expire after five minutes and are consumed once, even on verification failure. Sessions expire after seven days. Cookies are HTTP-only, SameSite=Lax, and Secure on HTTPS. API writes enforce the same origin; requests are rate limited. Biometric data and private keys remain with the authenticator.
+
+The email is an **unverified account identifier**, not an email recovery method. No password or email fallback is implemented. Add a second passkey or retain access to your synced passkey provider to avoid losing access. Café menu, orders, rewards, and location pages provide informational content; no ordering backend is included.
+
+## Automated verification
+
+```sh
+pnpm test
+pnpm check
+pnpm build
+```
+
+Integration tests use cryptographically signed synthetic authenticator responses against the real server and verifier, without a browser. They exercise registration, login, reauthentication, rename, additional keys, removal, logout, session revocation, ownership checks, last-key protection, single-use and expired challenges, invalid origins, signatures, signature counters, and missing user verification. Physical authenticator prompts depend on the browser, device, HTTPS origin, and provider; manual browser verification was intentionally omitted.
+
+WebAuthn implementation follows the [SimpleWebAuthn server documentation](https://simplewebauthn.dev/docs/packages/server/) and [passkey guidance](https://simplewebauthn.dev/docs/advanced/passkeys).
